```c
int flb_msgpack_to_json(char *json_str, size_t json_size, const msgpack_object *obj)
{
    int ret = -1;
    int off = 0;
    if (json_str == NULL || obj == NULL) {
        return -1;
    }
    ret = msgpack2json(json_str, &off, json_size - 1, obj);
    json_str[off] = '\0';
    return ret ? off: ret;
}
```

`msgpack2json` is called with `left = json_size - 1`. Inside, `try_to_write` ensures `*off + str_len < left`. So `*off < left = json_size - 1`. So `json_str[off]` where `off < json_size - 1` is within bounds. Safe.

But wait, `try_to_write_str` (flb_utils_write_str) has a DIFFERENT bounds check. It checks `available <= required` where `available = size - *off` and `size = left = json_size - 1`. So `available = json_size - 1 - *off`. And the per-iteration checks. So `flb_utils_write_str` writes at most `available` bytes = `json_size - 1 - *off` bytes. So `*off` after writing is at most `json_size - 1`. Then `json_str[off]` where `off <= json_size - 1` is within bounds (buffer is `json_size` bytes). Safe.

